course: dictionary
lesson: 11266
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can find out which one the author means by reading the words around it. Check each possible meaning against the sentence and see which one fits.
explain: «Longitude was so hard that ships were wrecked for centuries.» Here wrecked means badly damaged or destroyed, because ships were broken by the sea. A wreck can also be a thing: the broken ship itself, which divers may explore.
explain: «The rain wrecked our picnic.» The rain did not break anything, so wrecked means spoiled. Try it: swap in ruined and the sentence still makes sense. «I'm a wreck» is informal and means very tired, ill or upset.
fact: Wreck can be a noun (a wreck) or a verb (to wreck something).
fact: A wreck can be a ship, car, plane or building that has been badly damaged or destroyed.
fact: To wreck something can also mean to spoil plans or chances, as in «The rain wrecked our picnic.»
fact: Informal use: «I'm a wreck» means I am very tired, ill or upset.
fact: The w in wreck is silent, so you say it like "reck".
exercise: «Longitude was so hard that ships were wrecked for centuries.» What does wrecked mean here? | Very tired | *Badly damaged or destroyed | Spoiled a picnic | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «Divers explored the wreck on the seabed.» In this sentence, what is a wreck? | *A ship that was badly damaged and sank | A sandy beach | A new, shiny boat | A type of fish
exercise: «The kids wrecked the room.» What does wrecked mean here? | Painted it blue | Locked the door | Cleaned it very carefully | *Made a big mess
test: «The rain wrecked our picnic.» Which word could replace wrecked? | Painted | Measured | *Spoiled | Admired
test: «I'm a wreck after the long walk.» What does a wreck mean here? | *Very tired | A broken ship | Happy and bouncy | A new car
exam: Which sentence uses wreck as a verb? | Divers swam around the wreck | The old wreck lay rusting on the beach | I am a wreck today | *The storm may wreck the harbour
